Oracle-only source: floating-target-environment.c
Command: CC -std=gnu11 -O0/-O2 -ffp-contract=off SOURCE -o /tmp/fp-probe
All four executions exit 0 with identical output. Instruction operand order
is pinned by inline assembly; this is not a claim about every C optimizer's
choice of NaN operand order. Fields below are exact binary64 representations.
Initial FPCR=0; FLT_EVAL_METHOD=0, FLT_RADIX=2; sizeof float/double/long double=4/8/16.
FPCR=0:
q1-q2 add=7ff8000000000011 mul=7ff8000000000011 div=7ff8000000000011 neg=fff8000000000011
q2-q1 add=fff8000000000022 mul=fff8000000000022 div=fff8000000000022 neg=7ff8000000000022
q1-s2 add=fff8000000000022 mul=fff8000000000022 div=fff8000000000022 neg=fff8000000000011
s1-q2 add=7ff8000000000011 mul=7ff8000000000011 div=7ff8000000000011 neg=fff0000000000011
s1-s2 add=7ff8000000000011 mul=7ff8000000000011 div=7ff8000000000011 neg=fff0000000000011
zero-zero add=0000000000000000 mul=0000000000000000 div=7ff8000000000000 neg=8000000000000000
smallest-two add=4000000000000000 mul=0000000000000002 div=0000000000000000 neg=8000000000000001
round-nearest=3ff0000000000000
FPCR=(1<<22): round-upward=3ff0000000000001
FPCR=(1<<24): smallest-two mul=0 div=0; sign-only neg retains 8000000000000001
FPCR=(1<<25): q1-q2 add/mul/div=7ff8000000000000; neg retains fff8000000000011
Original FPCR is restored before exit; no EvoCC FPCR manipulation is implemented.

Candidate-engine qualification: floating-engine-nan.rs, compiled with rustc
--edition 2021 --extern rustc_apfloat=<pinned rlib> -L dependency=target/frontend/debug/deps.
For q1-q2 and s1-q2, add/mul/div retain/quiet q1 as the hardware probe does.
For q1-s2, all three unwrapped engine operations return 7ff8000000000011,
NOT the hardware probe's fff8000000000022. Runtime target adaptation is required.
The production finite_binary API rejects NaN inputs, so it does not expose
this difference as accepted target arithmetic. This is not an upstream IEEE
conformance accusation or a passing full-engine qualification.

Target header census: CC -std=gnu11 -dM -E -include float.h -include math.h
-include fenv.h -x c /dev/null, same fixed container.
Both: FE_TONEAREST=0, FE_UPWARD=0x400000, FE_DOWNWARD=0x800000,
FE_TOWARDZERO=0xc00000; FP_NAN/INFINITE/ZERO/SUBNORMAL/NORMAL=0/1/2/3/4.
GCC FLT_ROUNDS=1; Clang FLT_ROUNDS=(__builtin_flt_rounds()).
The combined headers define __STDC_IEC_559__=1 and __STDC_IEC_60559_BFP__=201404L.
Those reference capability macros do not establish EvoCC Annex F support;
reconciling the capability overlay remains in FP10 before runtime admission.

Exploratory non-ICE file-scope array bound (not the final arithmetic fixture):
